WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:06.680
Film Courage: Tom, one of your scripts that
you had from the 2006 era on ended up getting

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:08.620
you a manager?

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:10.150
Is that right?

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:13.209
Tom Oesch, Director of DANGER ONE:
Yes, that was is 2012.

00:00:13.209 --> 00:00:17.050
So it was a few years into making stuff.

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:26.480
The way I got the managers…well I probably
have to rewind, I went to USC Film School.

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:30.560
In film school (any film school) you make
a lot of friends and they are the ones that

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:35.690
help you make stuff once you’re out of school
and they help get you jobs and that also connect

00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:36.690
you.

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:47.140
So in 2012 I was with a writer/producer friend,
we were working on this for a teen conspiracy

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:50.260
thriller and it took place in Napa.

00:00:50.260 --> 00:01:00.899
And while it was being written I was producing
a fake trailer to help pitch it, to help show

00:01:00.899 --> 00:01:05.650
what the movie would look and feel like.

00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:15.350
And half of it…no probably a quarter of
it was like a so-called script-o-matic, so

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:18.850
we would rip shots from existing movies.

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:27.650
So then the other 70% we would actually shoot,
we got locations and hired actors, we wrote

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:30.590
the script for a trailer.

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:37.049
We went out and shot those elements and cut
it all together, color-timed it so it’s

00:01:37.049 --> 00:01:41.290
all very consistent, had it mixed at a friend’s
post-production house.

00:01:41.290 --> 00:01:47.180
And so we had a trailer that looked…a lot
of people were actually confused.

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:51.180
They thought this was a movie that had already
gotten made.

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:56.409
Also one of the actresses in the film was
Aimee Teegarden.

00:01:56.409 --> 00:02:02.690
Before she became a well-known actress she
was actually the lead actress in my thesis

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:03.860
film at USC.

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:06.149
So that’s how she and I met and became friends.

00:02:06.149 --> 00:02:10.890
So she agreed to be in this trailer just because
people recognize her.

00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:17.940
So when the trailer was sent out we got a
little bit more attention than we would if

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:19.970
she wasn’t in it.

00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:25.120
So one of my friends from USC was friends
with a manager.

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:29.860
He sent this manager the trailer, they’re
like “this looks like someone we can get

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:33.790
made” and that’s how I got my first manager.

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:40.040
And that’s also how I met the writer who
wrote DANGER ONE Steffen because they were

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:43.270
managing him at the same time too.

00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:50.560
So even though that teen conspiracy thriller
never got made it eventually got to DANGER

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:56.170
ONE getting made just because it introduced
Steffen and we became friends and realized

00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:57.550
we worked together well.

